                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi>
                </head>
                <p>The First Committee of the United Nations General Assembly met for the second
                    time on September 30 between 3 and 6 p.m.; for the record, see U.N. document
                    A/C.1/SR.347. The Soviet draft resolution (A/C.1/562) inviting both
                    representatives of North and South Korea to participate in the debate was
                    rejected by a vote of 46 to 6, with 7 abstentions. The Chinese resolution
                    (A/C.1/563) inviting a representative of the Republic of Korea to participate
                    without the right to vote was accepted by a vote of 50 to 5, with 5 abstentions.
                    The Committee then took up the eight-power draft resolution on Korea
                    (A/C.1/558), the text of which is contained in telegram Delga 57, September 29, from New York, <ref
                        target="#pg826">page 826</ref>. In the course of the discussion, Ambassador
                    Austin made a statement wherein he referred to the 38th Parallel as an
                    artificial barrier having no basis for existence in law or reason and endorsed
                    the resolution’s goal of a unified and independent Korea. For the text of
                    Ambassador Austin’s remarks, see Department of State <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bulletin</hi>, October 9, 1950, page 579.</p>
            </div>
